---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description } from '../../content';

const path = '/guides/email-sms-coordination';
const title = 'Email + SMS Coordination Playbook';
const desc = description(site, 'guides/email-sms-coordination');


---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a href="/guides" class="link">← All SMS guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-8">
      <h2>Choose one owner for each customer intent</h2>
      <p>A cart event is one customer decision even when two tools receive it. Give that decision an owner, a cancellation condition and a fallback. Email can carry comparison details; SMS may suit an opted-in request for timely availability. Sending both automatically does not prove either adds value. This guide is for lifecycle managers reconciling separate email and SMS systems, or separate workflows inside one platform.</p>
      <h2>Write an arbitration table before enabling flows</h2>
      <p>For each intent record the event identifier, primary channel, secondary channel condition, expiry, purchase cancellation and responsible operator. For cart recovery, a proposed rule is email first, with SMS considered only while the cart is still actionable and the subscriber remains eligible. For requested stock alerts, availability may own priority. These are design choices to test, not universal waiting periods. Keep service notifications in a separate policy so a promotion cannot borrow their urgency.</p>
      <h2>Keep permissions and identity separate</h2>
      <p>Link email, phone and Shopify customer ID only using a reliable identity relationship. Shared household phones, guest checkout and changed numbers need an exception path. A valid email subscription does not establish SMS permission. Maintain channel-specific status and disclosure provenance; do not overwrite a revoked phone subscription when a customer updates an email address. Resolve ambiguous merges before activating the linked profile.</p>
      <h2>Cancel queued work when the customer state changes</h2>
      <p>Recheck purchase, consent, inventory, offer validity and support status at dispatch, not just at flow entry. Give the same event a deduplication key so retries cannot create a second recovery sequence. If a purchase arrives late, cancel remaining promotional steps and record why. An email open is an unreliable reason to treat a sale as complete; use an appropriate commerce event. When event freshness is unknown, hold the dependent promotion rather than guessing.</p>
      <h2>Design fallback as a new eligibility decision</h2>
      <p>A failed email is not permission to text. A blocked SMS should not automatically generate another promotion by email. Check the fallback channel permission, whether the offer is still useful, and whether another workflow already owns the intent. Keep a shared contact ledger with planned, cancelled, submitted and failed states. Do not count a planned message against delivery metrics or treat submission as a confirmed delivery.</p>
      <h2>Proposed collision cases for a staging review</h2>
      <p>Use controlled fixtures before enabling the workflow: the same cart event arrives twice; purchase arrives after enrollment but before dispatch; a person unsubscribes while a message waits; inventory becomes unavailable; two customer IDs share a phone; the event feed stops. Expected outcomes should specify cancellation or a hold and identify the audit entry. These are proposed acceptance cases, not tests performed for this article. Have the operator inspect both tools, not only the automation canvas.</p>
      <h2>Choose a stack by its observable handoffs</h2>
      <p>A combined platform can reduce synchronization work, while separate tools can fit different teams and sending requirements. Either design needs event freshness, cancellation and suppression visibility. Ask suppliers to demonstrate a single cart through both channels with a late purchase event. Compare permissions, retry behavior, audit export and migration reversibility before price. Browse the app catalog for candidates; no ordering here certifies a winner.</p>
      <h2>Where Sequenzy fits</h2>
      <p>Sequenzy advertises email automation alongside a paid-plan SMS/MMS beta using a US/Canada toll-free number. That makes a combined workflow a candidate to evaluate; it does not establish that your Shopify events, cancellation rules or external SMS handoffs work. Request a demonstration using the arbitration table above before replacing an existing sender. See the current supplier scope in its pricing page.</p>
      <h2>Measure the customer decision once</h2>
      <p>Use order IDs to deduplicate commerce outcomes across vendor reports. If you run a proposed experiment, assign eligible customers consistently to email alone or the coordinated policy, hold the offer constant, and compare contribution after messaging and discount costs. Review unsubscribes and support complaints alongside orders. Two attribution dashboards claiming one purchase do not create two purchases. Keep the treatment definition and event failures in the experiment notes.</p>
      <h2>Sources and related decisions</h2>
      <p>Supplier documentation checked November 25, 2026: <a href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">Sequenzy current product scope</a>. Product claims are scoped to these supplier statements; implementation and jurisdiction-specific requirements still need review.</p>
      <p><a href="/guides/segmentation">Segmentation</a> · <a href="/guides/two-way-sms">Two Way Sms</a> · <a href="/apps">App catalog</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
